WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.915
Effective review traces source,
transformation, and sink.

2
00:00:03.995 --> 00:00:07.744
Sources include request values, API
responses, database fields,

3
00:00:07.824 --> 00:00:12.109
URL state, postMessage events, browser
storage, and third-party content.

4
00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:16.407
A stored field is not trusted merely
because it came from the application's

5
00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:19.366
database; its origin and allowed use still
matter.

6
00:00:19.446 --> 00:00:21.723
Identify the parser invoked at the sink,

7
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:25.619
then verify that every transformation
matches that exact context.

8
00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:28.913
Inventory HTML-parsing APIs, raw framework
rendering,

9
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:32.073
dynamic script construction,
string-to-code calls,

10
00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:36.973
security-sensitive URL assignments, and
template helpers that suppress escaping.

11
00:00:37.053 --> 00:00:40.133
Review alternate paths: server and client
rendering,

12
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:45.101
desktop and mobile views, previews and
exports, initial hydration and later updates.

13
00:00:45.181 --> 00:00:49.533
A value safely handled in one component
can reach a different sink elsewhere.

14
00:00:49.613 --> 00:00:54.166
Central wrappers help only when their
names state the context and callers cannot

15
00:00:54.246 --> 00:00:56.522
bypass the security decision casually.

16
00:00:56.602 --> 00:00:59.481
Build regression tests around intended
behavior.

17
00:00:59.561 --> 00:01:03.645
Confirm that special HTML characters
remain visible text in text paths,

18
00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:06.002
unexpected URL protocols are rejected,

19
00:01:06.082 --> 00:01:09.764
malformed rich content is processed under
the sanitizer policy,

20
00:01:09.844 --> 00:01:13.928
and raw-HTML paths accept only values
created by the approved boundary.

21
00:01:14.008 --> 00:01:18.896
Test client and server renderers
separately and exercise post-processing that might

22
00:01:18.976 --> 00:01:20.315
undo a transformation.

23
00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:25.015
Keep fixtures focused on policy properties
instead of accumulating an offensive

24
00:01:25.095 --> 00:01:26.032
string catalog.

25
00:01:26.112 --> 00:01:28.589
Add browser controls as defense-in-depth.

26
00:01:28.669 --> 00:01:33.222
A well-designed Content Security Policy
can constrain scripts and other active

27
00:01:33.302 --> 00:01:37.922
resources, but it does not correct an
unsafe sink and requires careful deployment

28
00:01:38.002 --> 00:01:38.939
and monitoring.

29
00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:43.907
Trusted Types enforcement can require
approved typed values at covered DOM injection

30
00:01:43.987 --> 00:01:47.402
sinks, concentrating risky conversions in
named policies.

31
00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:49.959
It does not automatically sanitize inputs;

32
00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:53.052
an unsafe policy can still authorize
unsafe content.

33
00:01:53.132 --> 00:01:57.819
Evaluate browser support and rollout
needs, and restrict who can create policies.

34
00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:02.652
HttpOnly can prevent script access to
selected cookies if script execution occurs,

35
00:02:02.732 --> 00:02:07.084
but it neither prevents XSS nor protects
every action available in the user's

36
00:02:07.164 --> 00:02:07.700
session.
